  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nToday through Thursday: Widespread Rainfall\n\nRain is forecast to continue across Minnesota, Wisconsin, and \nIowa from today through Thursday. An upper level trough and low\npressure system are continuing to move east through mid week. An\nH500 center of low pressure will trek northeast and is likely to\nrest over the Upper Midwest on Wednesday into Thursday. Model \nensemble guidance depicts this scenario well with the newest \nforecast cycle.\n\nThe pattern mentioned above will lead to multiple rounds of \nshowers with embedded thunderstorms, with the first round \nalready underway at the time of this writing. The latest HREF \ncontinues to depict the areas of heaviest rainfall from today \nthrough Thursday to the northwest of the region (southeast ND \ninto northern MN) and an additional area to the south (mainly \ncentral/eastern IA). However, the southern area of higher rain \ntotals may still extend into the CWA, especially if scenarios \nsuch as the RAP, NAM, and NAMNest pan out, which result in a \nwidespread 1 inch or more of rainfall during this time frame. \nHREF and REFS mean rainfall totals through Wednesday morning \nstill depict a widespread 0.75-1 inch, with the highest totals \nfor our forecast area south of I-90 where there is a 10-30 \npercent chance for rainfall totals to exceed 2 inches. South of \nI-90 is also the area that has the highest potential for thunder\n(roughly 10-30% according to the NBM) so if any convective \nprocesses can be achieved, they could lead to slightly heavier \nrainfall rates.\n\nGiven the aforementioned low pressure system slows down and \nremains over the Upper Midwest into Thursday, wrap around \nmoisture should lead to continued chances for showers. Forecast\nmodels are in good agreement that the surface low will stay \njust to the northwest or north of the forecast area on Thursday,\nso the northern half of the area will have the higher chances \nfor rainfall during the day. As the synoptic and surface lows \nexit to the northeast on Thursday afternoon into the evening, \nthe rain potential will decrease from west to east going into \nFriday morning. Temperatures today and Tuesday should top out in\nthe 60s, which is above average for early November. However, \nthose temperatures will begin to decrease on Wednesday and \nThursday as the upper level low wraps colder air in over the \nDriftless Region.\n\nFriday into the Weekend: Trending Drier and Cooler\n\nZonal flow takes over on Saturday before another trough digs \nsouth into the Upper Midwest during the second half of this next\nweekend. This trough may bring additional chances (15-20%) for \nprecipitation late Saturday into Sunday after a mostly dry day \non Friday. The trough will also usher in a secondary, \nreinforcing cooler airmass that will keep temperatures down near\naverage next weekend.",
